1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,574
One hundred sixty-two point five five zero
megahertz.

2
00:00:05,494 --> 00:00:11,286
Tune there, almost anywhere in the United States, and
you'll hear the robotic voice of a weather station.

3
00:00:11,206 --> 00:00:19,157
That number — the one thing everybody knows about a radio signal
— is its frequency: how many times the wave cycles each second.

4
00:00:19,077 --> 00:00:21,455
Get it right and the signal is yours.

5
00:00:21,375 --> 00:00:23,997
Get it wrong by a hair and you hear
nothing at all.

6
00:00:24,147 --> 00:00:33,932
Frequency is measured in hertz — one hertz is one cycle per second, named for Heinrich
Hertz, who first generated and detected radio waves in the eighteen-eighties.

7
00:00:33,852 --> 00:00:44,206
It's the inverse of the cycle's duration: a signal at one hundred megahertz
repeats a hundred million times a second, so each cycle lasts just ten nanoseconds.

8
00:00:44,356 --> 00:00:47,466
Picture two waves side by side for one
second.

9
00:00:47,386 --> 00:00:55,766
The low-frequency wave fits a handful of cycles into that
second; the high-frequency wave packs in thousands of times more.

10
00:00:55,686 --> 00:01:00,236
Same speed of travel — always the speed of
light — just a faster wiggle.

11
00:01:00,156 --> 00:01:09,976
And since radio numbers get big fast, we scale the unit: kilohertz for
thousands, megahertz for millions, gigahertz for billions of cycles per second.

12
00:01:10,126 --> 00:01:16,161
Frequency has a twin: wavelength — the
physical distance one cycle covers in flight.

13
00:01:16,081 --> 00:01:25,727
Because every radio wave travels at the same speed, the two are locked
on a seesaw: wavelength equals the speed of light divided by frequency.

14
00:01:25,647 --> 00:01:28,641
Double the frequency and the wavelength
halves.

15
00:01:28,561 --> 00:01:39,995
An FM broadcast station at one hundred megahertz has waves three meters long; a UHF
public-safety channel near four hundred sixty megahertz, about sixty-five centimeters.

16
00:01:39,915 --> 00:01:46,472
That's why antennas come in different sizes — an antenna wants to
match its wave, and we'll spend a whole chapter on that later.

17
00:01:46,622 --> 00:01:53,250
Lay every possible frequency along one axis and you get
the radio spectrum — and its neighborhoods have names.

18
00:01:53,170 --> 00:01:58,044
HF, the shortwave bands that bounce off
the upper atmosphere and cross oceans.

19
00:01:57,964 --> 00:02:02,374
VHF, home of FM broadcast, aircraft, and
marine radio.

20
00:02:02,294 --> 00:02:08,004
UHF, where most trunked public-safety
systems, cell phones, and Wi-Fi live.

21
00:02:07,924 --> 00:02:13,530
A frequency isn't just a number — it's an
address, with physics and regulations attached.

22
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:20,551
For an SDR, frequency is the first thing you set: it
decides what slice of spectrum lands in the capture.

23
00:02:20,471 --> 00:02:27,459
But here's the catch — cheap tuner hardware runs slightly
off its dial frequency, by a few parts per million.

24
00:02:27,379 --> 00:02:38,592
GopherTrunk, like any serious decoder, applies a correction and then continuously tracks the
residual error while it decodes, because at these precisions, oscillators drift with temperature.

25
00:02:38,512 --> 00:02:43,491
Getting the frequency right, and keeping it
right, is the first requirement for any decode.

26
00:02:44,441 --> 00:02:53,959
So: frequency is cycles per second, it's chained to wavelength through the speed
of light, and it places every signal into a named neighborhood of the spectrum.

27
00:02:53,879 --> 00:02:55,747
Full write-up linked below.

